Title: EP05 — 바이브코딩이 뭔데?
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibecode Town Season 1
Source: Standalone entry-point (evergreen/help)
Draft date: 2026-03-18
Duration: 3:30-4:00
Notes: v5 (6-segment format, narrator-only, 2D flat vector, Vee solo). First standalone episode — not connected to EP01-04 arc. Target search: "what is vibe coding"

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:15]                                       */
/* emotion_curve: CURIOSITY → RECOGNITION                            */
/* narrative_stage: HOOK                                              */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: whiteboard */
/* shorts_candidate */

= A search bar appears center frame. Someone types: "vibe coding." Autocomplete explodes: "vibe coding tutorial," "vibe coding is it real," "vibe coding vs programming," "vibe coding dead?"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seventy thousand people googled this last month. Half of them think it's the future. The other half think it's a joke.

= The search results split into two columns. Left: "Vibe coding will replace developers." Right: "Vibe coding is not real coding." Both glow red.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They're both wrong.

> TITLE CARD: **바이브코딩이 뭔데?**

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 2: MISCONCEPTION [0:15-0:45]                              */
/* emotion_curve: OVERCONFIDENCE → FALSE CERTAINTY                   */
/* narrative_stage: MISCONCEPTION                                    */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY

= Vee sits at her desk. She types a single sentence into an AI chat: "Make me a weather app." A complete app appears on her screen. Colorful. Functional. Instant.

/* vee_reaction: 1/6 */

= Vee leans back, arms behind her head. Smug nod. She gestures at the screen as if to say: "See? Done."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's what most people think vibe coding is. You type what you want. AI builds it. You ship it. Congratulations — you're a developer now.

/* visual_type: motion_graphic | space: whiteboard */

= A conveyor belt animation. A person drops a sentence in one end. A finished product rolls out the other. Simple. Clean. Effortless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Just tell the AI what to do. It's coding. It's easy. It's basically autopilot.

= The conveyor belt runs smoothly. Products roll off. Everything shines.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Right?

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 3: THE_CRACK [0:45-1:15]                                  */
/* emotion_curve: FALSE CERTAINTY → DOUBT                            */
/* narrative_stage: THE_CRACK                                        */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

= Vee opens the weather app she just built. She taps a city name. The temperature shows 847 degrees. She taps another city. The app crashes.

/* vee_reaction: 2/6 */

= Vee stares at the screen. She opens the AI chat again. She wants to fix it. She types: "Fix the bug." The AI responds with a wall of code. Vee reads it. Reads it again. She has no idea what changed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She told AI to build it. It built something. She told AI to fix it. It fixed something. But she can't explain what she asked for. And she can't explain what she got back.

= The chat window fills with messages. Vee scrolling up. Scrolling down. Each message is a new instruction, each response a new version. None of them connect.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's the crack. It's not that AI can't code. It's that she doesn't know what she told it to do.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 4: CORE [1:15-3:00]                                       */
/* emotion_curve: DOUBT → CLARITY                                    */
/* narrative_stage: CORE                                              */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */
/* extended_metaphor_start */

= TRANSITION: Desk fades. Clean white background. Two people appear side by side.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Let's compare two people.

= LEFT: A person sits at a piano. Sheet music on the stand. Fingers on keys. They play — notes come out. Simple melody.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Person A plays piano. They read sheet music. Their fingers press keys. Music comes out. That's a skill. Nobody argues.

= RIGHT: A person stands in front of an orchestra. No instrument. They hold a baton. They raise it. The entire orchestra plays — a massive, complex symphony.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Person B conducts an orchestra. They don't play a single instrument. But the symphony only exists because they know what it should sound like.

/* shorts_candidate */

= The conductor waves the baton. The music swells. Then they stop — one hand up. Silence. They point at the violins. The violins adjust. The conductor nods. Music resumes, better than before.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The conductor's job isn't pressing keys. It's holding the entire piece in their head — every instrument, every timing, every transition — and communicating that vision to the performers.

/* extended_metaphor_end */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The orchestra fades. Text appears center frame: "Vibe Coding = Conducting"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That's vibe coding. You're not writing code. You're conducting AI. The skill isn't syntax. The skill is knowing what the system should do — and communicating that clearly enough that AI can execute it.

= Below, three pillars draw themselves: "INTENT" / "STRUCTURE" / "VERIFICATION"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Intent — what am I building and why. Structure — where does each piece live. Verification — how do I know it works. That's it. Those three things.

/* vee_reaction: 3/6 */
/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

= Quick flash: Vee's eyes widen behind her glasses. Recognition. Not eureka — more like "oh, that's what I've been missing." 1.5 seconds.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= A spectrum line appears. Left end: "Prompt Engineering." Right end: "System Thinking." A marker slides from left toward the right and stops at "System Thinking."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
People call it prompt engineering. It's not. Prompt engineering is about phrasing — saying the right words to get a good response. Vibe coding is system thinking — understanding how pieces connect before you ask AI to build any of them.

= The "Prompt Engineering" label shrinks. "System Thinking" glows.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A prompt engineer asks: "How do I get better output?" A vibe coder asks: "What should the system look like when it's done?"

/* vee_reaction: 4/6 */

= Vee pulls out a notebook. Not a terminal. A notebook. She starts sketching boxes and arrows. System diagram. No code.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Vee stopped typing prompts. She started drawing systems.

/* shorts_candidate */

= CU on the notebook. Three boxes: "Data In" → "Process" → "Data Out." Below each box, a single question: "From where?" / "What rules?" / "To whom?"

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Three boxes. Three questions. Before she writes a single prompt, she knows what she's building. That's the difference between telling AI to code and actually vibe coding.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 5: REFRAME [3:00-3:30]                                    */
/* emotion_curve: CLARITY → CONVICTION                               */
/* narrative_stage: REFRAME                                          */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= Zoom out. The spectrum, the three pillars, and the orchestra — all visible as small icons on a canvas.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's what nobody wants to admit. Vibe coding is harder than regular coding. Not because the tools are complex. Because you have to think about the whole system before any of it exists.

= The conveyor belt from MISCONCEPTION reappears — but now it's transparent. Inside, you can see the mess: tangled gears, broken belts, misaligned parts.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The "just tell AI" approach works for demos. It doesn't work for anything you need to maintain, debug, or explain to another human.

/* vee_reaction: 5/6 */

= Vee closes the AI chat window. Opens her notebook instead. She nods slowly. Determined.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Vibe coding is a real skill. It's system thinking expressed through AI. And like any real skill — it takes practice.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 6: OUTRO_CTA [3:30-3:55]                                  */
/* emotion_curve: CONVICTION → INVITATION                            */
/* narrative_stage: OUTRO_CTA                                        */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S ROOM - DAY (CONTINUOUS)

= Vee at her desk. The notebook is open beside her keyboard. She types a prompt — but this time, it's different. It's structured. It references the notebook. It has context.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Next time someone asks you what vibe coding is — it's not "telling AI to code." It's knowing what to build, and conducting the AI to build it.

/* vee_reaction: 6/6 */

= Vee glances at the camera — the first time she's ever looked directly at the viewer. A tiny knowing nod. Then back to her screen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Start with three boxes and three questions. The rest follows.

> END CARD: 구독 + "다음 편: 10분만에 AI 에이전트"

= FADE TO BLACK.

> END
